  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "Let \\(P(n)\\) and \\(S(n)\\) denote the product and the sum, respectively, of the digits of the integer \\(n.\\) For example, \\(P(23)=6\\) and \\(S(23)=5.\\) Suppose \\(N\\) is a two-digit number such that \\(N=P(N)+S(N).\\) What is the units digit of \\(N?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(8\\)",
    "E": "\\(9\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Let \\(a\\) be the tens digit and \\(b\\) the units digit. Then \\(1\\le a\\le9\\), \\(0\\le b\\le9\\), and\n\\[\nN=10a+b,\\qquad P(N)=ab,\\qquad S(N)=a+b.\n\\]\nSubstitute these expressions into the given condition:\n\\[\n10a+b=ab+a+b.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(a+b\\) from both sides gives \\(9a=ab\\). Since a two-digit number has \\(a\\ne0\\), division by \\(a\\) is valid and gives \\(b=9\\).\n\nThis digit works for every allowed \\(a\\), because \\(9a+(a+9)=10a+9\\). Thus the units digit is \\(\\boxed{9}\\), choice E.",
